  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Widespread showers and seasonably cool temperatures persist \nthrough the rest of the week and into early next week.\n\n-  Near-critical fire weather continues for southwest Montana on\n   Friday due to gusty winds and low humidity.\n\nNo significant changes in the forecast. The significant low\npressure system is continuing to move into the Pacific Northwest\nwhich will cause southwesterly flow over the Northern Rockies.\nThis flow pattern will keep a decent fetch of moisture streaming\ninto the region tonight into Friday. Most locations will\nexperience decent precipitation amounts over the next 24 to 48\nhours, with north central Idaho receiving the bulk of the\nrainfall. The low is expected to make its way into the Northern\nRockies Sunday into Tuesday time frame causing an increase in the\nprecipitation coverage. The uncertainty in the forecast comes in \nthe amount of subtropical moisture that gets pulled into the low. \nTemperatures will remain below normal for this time of year. \n\nThe exception to the impacts from this low pressure system will be\nthe eastern half of Fire Weather Zone 111 (Madison County, MT) \nwhich will still experience warm, dry and breezy conditions on \nFriday afternoon creating near critical fire weather conditions. \n\nThe longer term models are showing a transitory ridge of high\npressure sliding over the region by the latter part of next week\nwhich will bring a warming and drying trend to the Northern\nRockies.",
